{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/1657-solars-as-npcs-old-forum-salvage","id":1657,"title":"Solars as NPCs (old forum salvage)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Solar Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-03T04:51:23-08:00","id":1657,"title":"Solars as NPCs (old forum salvage)","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 4:05:55 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Solars are the default Exalted and they have some serious prescribed ideas as to how to 'build' your character. Especially with castes acting as the skill backbone for development of powers.<br /> <br /> That said, Solars do offer some benefits that other Exalted do not:<br /> <br /> <i>1. No predisposed social groups, no Silver Pact, Dragon Blooded host, Deathlord mentors, Yu-Shan. They are on their own and build from the ground up.<br /> <br /> 2. Wider mix in groups. Solars can come from ANYWHERE in Creation at all strata of life. Playing the prince and the pauper works in this game.<br /> <br /> 3. Maximal Skill Flexibility. Solars just get nicer more general bonuses to skill development Especially in 3E. Pick up 1 charm for an Ability and you get the Excellency? Hell yes.</i><br /> <br /> But the same benefits also have drawbacks:<br /> <br /> <i>1. No support structures. Lack of allies outside of what you buy in chargen.<br /> <br /> 2. Wild mix of cultures can sometimes make silly groups who in the case of a few games don't share common language! (What do you mean you don't know Riverspeak? Why are we travelling together?&quot;)<br /> <br /> 3. Showy charms and effects. Solars are VERY iconic, and unless you are a Night caste, doing anything beyond subtle charms will be identifiable by people with enough Lore.</i><br /> <br /> But wen building a Solar there are some interesting ways to build, play, and handle them as NPCs. This thread is some of my more interesting ideas and suggestions that might help. I'll kick out with some character gen examples. But go into Solar Tips and Tricks. <b>And PLEASE if you have your own advice, post it.</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"></td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 4:24:05 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Justify the Caste:</b><br /> <br /> Here is a fun exercise. Take the baseline idea for a character. Job, background, etc. And disconnect your thoughts from caste and charms right away.<br /> <br /> Then sit down and write a few sentences justifying each of the 5 Solar castes and why he was chosen for it.<br /> <br /> <i>So: Bob the Scavenger Lord, born in Halta but moved with his family to Nexus at a young age, something of a dandy.<br /> <br /> <b>Dawn:</b></i> <i>Lead a revolt against the Dragon Blooded who were looting a local shrine and reformed his ways honoring the local gods, was exalted as Dawn because of his tactical thinking.<br /> <br /> <b>Zenith:</b></i> <i>Discovered old lore about the UCS and slowly converted to worshiping him and offered teachings and help to those the Immaculate Faith abandoned. Eventually was attacked for heresy and Exalted as Zenith.<br /> <br /> <b>Twilight:</b></i> <i>Slowly learned more and more 1st Age crafting techniques by studying his finds and began working on teaching manuals to share with Nexus crafters. Attacked by Deathlord agents who wished to keep the secrets, secret. Caused him to Exalt as Twilight rogue researcher.<br /> <br /> <b>Night:</b></i> <i>Proved to be the best at getting into impossible tombs and locations. Slowly developed into a peerless acrobat and tomb raider. Came into a fight with rival Scavenger Lords over territory and discovered their illegal deals with Yozi. Attacked them using his skill and Exalted as Night.<br /> <br /> <b>Eclipse:</b></i> <i>Mastered the Scavenger Lord social scene and promoted more and more 'research' into lost cities and culture. Eventually began grand expeditions far afield and Exalted as Eclipse at the discovery of a lost city near the elemental pole. Much to the surprise of his fellow explorers, but his social skill smoothed it over...so far.</i><br /> <br /> I've found this trick helps break expectations on characters. Sneaky ones don't always have to be Night, nor do social ones have to be Eclipse. Take a profession and see what castes it can mix into.<br /> <br /> Sometimes I even intentionally go counter to the prescribed caste just to see the result.[/INDENT]</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 4:38:44 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>The &quot;Support&quot; Solar</b><br /> <br /> Playing the support Solar can be rewarding and it doesn't suffer the downside of playing say a Cleric or support Wizard in D&amp;D. One of the benefits is you tend to be a skill monkey and it's really ease to pick up followers that you can buff with gear and training.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind it's more than just playing a Solar Crafter. That's one potential method of supporting a Circle. But a true supporting Solar thinks in terms of logistics.<br /> <br /> For Example:<br /> <br /> Often a group of Solars is over optimized for Combat, be it social or physical. Rarely do they spend he points or time to develop a character who has contacts in multiple fields, followers to handle day to day issues, or has a mix of status and resources to get into and out of some situations.<br /> <br /> Mind you, Solars are optimized to develop these links and to build that support system since it's one of their favored tricks, but I've seen far too many Solar groups have to 'build up' over time and it really slows down their long term goals.<br /> <br /> Playing a Support Solar is about thinking about those longer term goals. Let the rest of the group deal with the day to day issues. You are the Force Multiplier. You handle the ground work at establishing a base of operations, you equip followers with weapons and gear, you plan out social events the team and sneak into and lay on the charm, you keep contact with the Guild contacts and work out contracts for later jobs and expeditions.<br /> <br /> Don't just be a Solar crater or a Solar social butterfly.<br /> <br /> Think of yourself as the Solar CEO. Adventure Inc. You make it happen.<br /> <br /> The meta-actions in a solar campaign are half the fun of playing a Solar. You just happen to specialize in it.<br /> <br /> And if you want to fight, it's not a big deal to pick up 1 or 2 charms and a mix of artifacts and gear you build or bought and enjoy the rest of the day.<br /> <br /> But you are damn sure the next town they come too you are back on the 'job' of making sure the group has food and lodgings as well as recruiting some more followers to get the next adventure done.[/INDENT]</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 4:50:58 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">]This all seems more PC-oriented than NPC-oriented, but still, good stuff.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 4:52:05 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>DeadManSleeping wrote:</b><br /> This all seems more PC-oriented than NPC-oriented, but still, good stuff.<br /> <br /> <br /> It's a mix. I've NPC stuff coming.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"></td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:52:51-08:00","id":1658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 5:25:12 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Solar NPC &quot;The Fixer&quot;</b> -<br /> <br /> Ever had a player with a less than impressive back story? Lack of decent motivation? It's not always their fault, some players never really had to deal with such an immerse world and some folks don't like their early thoughts on the setting to influence how a character develops. They take the tabula rasa idea of PC creation and go a little too far.<br /> <br /> Enter the &quot;Fixer&quot;. Now this concept doesn't have to be a Solar, but I find Solars work better for a few reasons. Here's how the fixer works.<br /> <br /> 1. The Fixer is modeled after the PC that needs a little work. So if they are a sneak thief Night caste, they are a lesser sneak thief, maybe with their ability points too spread out. The point is they have fewer charms (like 5 or so) and they JUST Exalted.<br /> <br /> 2. The Fixer has a LOT of bad NPC issues. Usually a Dynast is hunting them, or they owe a Deathlord favors, etc. The Fixer has backgrounds that jive with the underdefined PC motivation but are not exactly like them. So if the PC hates &quot;Deathlords&quot; the Fixer has a specific problem with Walker in Darkness. Etc.<br /> <br /> 3. The Fixer gravitates to the PC that needs help and treats him/her like a mentor. Acts civil, friendly, etc. I often find the fixer being a kid, or just younger than the PC helps them establish the bond. The trick is don't be obnoxious about it. The NPC is there to learn from the PC, and to offer backup. Nothing to obvious, just friendly help and maybe offers a favor or there for the PC training them.<br /> <br /> Once the Fixer is in place, it's time for them to do their work. There are a few ways to do this:<br /> <br /> 1. The Fixer expressly asks the PC for help against their background problems. Some players are open to this. And if the character spends a lot of time in game working to help the Fixer's issues I quietly suggest they update their Intimacies (motivations) to match the Fixer. Or maybe as a counterpoint to the fixer. If they are not down with revenge, then maybe they can offer more positive support. Either way, intimacies get updated and the PC gets more interesting. Point is the PC gets to play mentor and some players REALLY dig this.<br /> <br /> 2. The Fixer comes under attack. Backgrounds catch up to people. This is more a 'pull the whole group in&quot; solution and that's fine too. It adds complications to a game and gives the group a larger pantheon of baddies to deal with. The Fixer needs to be grateful and helpful to the group for their mentorship, and as always offer to allow people to build intimacies and change motivations.<br /> <br /> 3. The &quot;Death Vow&quot;. This is a last use option. If the NPC is beloved of the group...kill them. But do it in such a fashion the PC's get a chance to talk to them before they die. Especially the PC who the fixer is there to fix. The NPC asks them to vow to avenge them so they can die in piece, knowing their friend will carry out their last wishes. Save the Fixer's family, take out the NPC that killed them, etc etc. Solars die. NPC allies of the party dying needs to be a serious moment. Let the group ham it up. Fixer has done their job and needs to move on. Dying is one way to handle it, but not the only way.<br /> <br /> In all times, make the Fixer &quot;adoptable&quot;. Something the group can see a clear bonus to taking the NPC under their wing. Solars being on the run tends to gravitate to other Solars for protection and alliances. Use this!<br /> <br /> It works with other Exalts too. Adopting a Solar &quot;Fixer&quot; in a Sidereal game works too. Lunar, you name it. The weird switch of seeing a Solar, typically powerful and self assured, in a position of weakness tends to draw PCs to them like moths to a flame. The Fixer works wonders if use carefully.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 5:58:06 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">]<b>The &quot;Scout&quot; Solar -</b><br /> <br /> Some people are just better at finding problems. Some Solars can be built to do just that and it's a benefit to the circle they are a member of.<br /> <br /> When I say Scout I'm not just talking about a hunt in the forest, tracking NPCs on foot kind of character. That's a type of Scout, but the &quot;Scout&quot; I'm talking about fits into a weird mix of technical, social, and survival skills. One part investigator, one part tactician, dash of spymaster, one part human lightning rod the Scout is adept at locating problems, surviving their initial outbreak, and then reporting spotted weaknesses back to the group.<br /> <br /> A good Scout focuses on situational awareness. Enhanced vision, good reflexes, and a solid mix of identification and informational charms. Also they pick up contacts to spread their information network around. Good social networking skills allows for Social Hacking and discovering clues the rest of the party missed.<br /> <br /> Stuff like &quot;Know the Court&quot;, &quot;Keen Senses&quot;, etc. Is the first half of the build. The second half is loads of and loads of Lore, Occult, Bureaucracy, Socialize, and War. The &quot;knowledge&quot; skills that allow you to take what you learn and convert it into useful data for the rest of the party.<br /> <br /> Good scouts tend to avoid direct confrontations with foes with out their party, and pick up dodge, social get away, etc charms to back that up.<br /> <br /> Finally consider some secret message techniques so you can call for help and pass along intel to the group.<br /> <br /> A good scout character plays well in Night, Twilight, and Eclipse castes, but is no joke with a properly built Dawn or Zenith. And they are invaluable to a party who is overspecialized in 'doing' things and not so good at finding out what needs to be done.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 7:47:11 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <b>Solar NPC &quot;The Fixer&quot;</b> -<br /> <br /> <br /> I'm not sure why you chose this name, but in Shadowrun and I think some other things, a &quot;Fixer&quot; is a prominent NPC type. It does something totally different, though!</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 8:30:08 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>DeadManSleeping wrote:</b><br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <b>Solar NPC &quot;The Fixer&quot;</b> -<br /> <br /> <br /> I'm not sure why you chose this name, but in Shadowrun and I think some other things, a &quot;Fixer&quot; is a prominent NPC type. It does something totally different, though!<br /> <br /> <br /> I thought it was a better name than <b>&quot;Plot Pet&quot;</b>.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:54:16-08:00","id":1659,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: TCA666</b> - Monday, June 10, 2013 11:59:51 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Cool advice so far. I like to mull over castes, too - sometimes it's one of the last things I decide for the character, even. When making Shen the Bright Lion, for example, I observed that he had good leanings toward <i>all</i> of the castes - good MA and Thrown, good Presence, Integrity and Resistance, good Occult and Lore, good Athletics and Larceny and Stealth, good Bureaucracy and Socialize. It was ultimately Night that I went with, but he could easily have fit into any of the castes. Sometimes it's fun to make a Night Caste king, or a Zenith bandit, or an illiterate Twilight.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Vespero</b> - Tuesday, June 11, 2013 8:16:06 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Nice stuff, Mostlyjoe.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Wednesday, June 12, 2013 7:25:35 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><i>Even</i> <b><i>Bob The Farmer</i></b> <i>has a dark past.</i> - aka. Life Pathing!<br /> <br /> Have you had to cook up a character for a game and you JUST COULDN'T COOK UP A BACKSTORY?<br /> <br /> No problem.<br /> <br /> Say Hello To Life Paths!<br /> <br /> Introduced back in the day in games like Cyberpunk 2020 and carried forward in stuff like Smallville the idea is a randomly rolled up background. Mind you, today's variants focus more on building up inner party connections and that's fine too. But lets get generic. The idea is to write up 10 or so Events in vague enough terms you can flesh them out from there. Oh hell, here is an example:<br /> <b>Quote:</b><br /> Dark Past Table: Roll 1d10<br /> <br /> 1. The Loss - In your youth agents of (1-3 The Realm, 3-6 A Deathlord, 7-0 A Local God) murdered your family and sold you into slavery. You swore vengeance.<br /> <br /> 2. The Madness - Years ago you stumbled your way into a Wyld zone, either lured or lost the Fair Folk captured you and were just about to feed when you were saved by a mysterious stranger. To this day the Fair Lord seeks you as sport.<br /> <br /> 3. The Legionnaire - You were claimed from your local village by a Realm Legion looking for bodies. You've spent the last few years being drilled into a passable warrior, only to run away recently in a bid to return home. Only, home is now quite strange.<br /> <br /> 4. The Murder - A local god blood was tormenting your villiage and you and a few of the local youths banded together and attacked the Godblood hoping to drive him away. Only you wound up delivering a lethal blow. Now the local God is looking for murderer of his son and you have gone into exile to avoid your family getting punished.<br /> <br /> 5. The Beach - You awake on a local beach, two jade coins over your eyes, and a change of close laying on your chest. No other memories of your life remain. The only clue you have is non-local flowers that were in your hands.<br /> <br /> 6. The Changeling - One of your siblings went missing in their youth, only to 'return' a few days later. You've always known the child that came back isn't your sibling and you've been discovering a growing series of disquieting crimes the being has done as you grown older. Can you prove it?<br /> <br /> 7. The Grandfather - When you were young your grandfather was killed in a local forest. Everyone warned you from going out there, but you did because your secret was you talked and spent time with your grandfather's ghost. Recently he claims he found the people who killed him and begs you to do justice.<br /> <br /> 8. The Betrothed - When you were a borne a local god demanded you as their consort in payment for a favor for our family. You've been groomed over the years to server your new spouse, but recently discovered the god tricked your family into the agreement.<br /> <br /> 9. The Heir - You had a child in your youth but were separated from your lover and child due to scandal and family meddling. Recently you have heard your lover was murdered and your child has gone missing.<br /> <br /> 0. The Deal - You made a deal with a demon. Once your family was dying from a plague and a demon offered you a cure for some rather vague favors down the line. Now it's come sneaking around asking about you!<br /> <br /> <br /> So suddenly Bob the Farmer has a cool dark past.<br /> <br /> The trick is to write up a few of these when you feel creative. Like an idea notebook of stuff in lots of 10. Mix tables together and roll up for different life events. Like Your Past, Romance, Disaster, etc.<br /> <br /> It's almost as fun as making a character and you can reuse it!<br /> <br /> Have fun!</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Vi-Zet</b> - Wednesday, June 12, 2013 8:05:53 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Quote:</b><br /> 1. The Loss - In your youth agents of (1-3 The Realm, 3-6 A Deathlord, 7-0 A Local God) murdered your family and sold you into slavery. You swore vengeance.<br /> <br /> I presume, Guild should be in this list) After all, Bob the Farmer has much better chances to become a Guild slave than to attract attention of the Empire, ancient ghosts or spirits.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:55:00-08:00","id":1660,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Wednesday, June 12, 2013 8:53:57 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Vi-Zet wrote:</b><br /> <b>Quote:</b><br /> 1. The Loss - In your youth agents of (1-3 The Realm, 3-6 A Deathlord, 7-0 A Local God) murdered your family and sold you into slavery. You swore vengeance.<br /> <br /> I presume, Guild should be in this list) After all, Bob the Farmer has much better chances to become a Guild slave than to attract attention of the Empire, ancient ghosts or spirits.<br /> <br /> <br /> Well, I figured the Guild was who they sold them too, but you can make your own custom list elements. I just banged that out.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 2:14:58 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>The Last Sane Solar NPC</b> -<br /> <br /> This variant NPC is a rare thing. A Solar Elder! Specifically a Solar who saw the purge coming, or was isolated from his/her fellows and went underground to avoid murder. And was so good at it, to this day the Sidereals still have no clue where the Solar is. They know he or she is there, but can't move against them because of the cost involved.<br /> <br /> I call this NPC the Last Sane because that's what they are...barely. Sane. The idea is to grant players 2 opportunities. The first is to take a Solar stepped in isolation and paranoia of hiding for all these years, willing to approach a group of new Solars because of a desperate need to contact, allowing them to 'redeem the elder' as a plot. The second is to give them someone who can describe to them just what being a powerful Solar can be like, and warns them about the excesses of their fellows. (Something that few Solars hear about from a Solar perspective.)<br /> <br /> This has to be handled carefully though. I usually apply a few controls:<br /> <br /> 1. The Solar is not a crafter and doesn't know how to re-establish the full scope of 1st Age wonders. They might have a treasure trove of info for Twilights that want to rediscover things on their own. This is very specific to avoid shutting down that style of play.<br /> <br /> 2. The Great Curse DID effect them, making them paranoid and overly secretive, when it comes down to it the Elder will allow the new generation of Solars to be the BIG DAMN HEROES. This avoids overshadowing the players.<br /> <br /> 3. The Elder doesn't have all the answers. After all, they've been out of touch for hundreds of years. They have a foggy but useful memory of what the 1st Age was like, but also remember the worst parts of it and especially how hubris caused the Solar fall. This allows the Solars to consider humility and the wider implications of their actions.<br /> <br /> 4. At most the Elder is essence 6. They didn't have a lot of time to meditate and build essence because they were being low key. They DO however have a wide range of Stealth, Perception, and Survival charms, with a healthy mix of what I'd consider a crazy loner Solar survivalist might have. Making them a wonderful teaching Patron.<br /> <br /> The key is to have the elder listen to what the players know about the world at large NOW. To the Elder everythign is new and weird, they know the small safe place they call home (maybe a pocket in elsewhere, or somewhere in the Wyld), and they have their memories. Play up the quiet mentor side of things. They offer advice, and will point them at old Solar caches of resources and intel. But they won't outshine the group. When the players catch in raw Charm development and Essence down the line in the campaign, the Elder might feel more at ease and come out in the open WITH THE GROUP as a more active Ally. Especially when negotiating with other Elders.<br /> <br /> Think of the NPC as a diplomat mentor.<br /> <br /> Also, it's a great tool to show just how much the great curse eats at the mind. The Elder is 'sane' but that's a whisper away from showing the cracks in their mind.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 2:30:08 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>The Con Artist Solar -</b><br /> <br /> There is a sucker born every minute, and having someone in the group who can exploit this actually goes a LONG way to handling complications that come up in places. Unlike a pure social character a true con artists has a mix of skills that include Performance, Presence, Larceny, Forgery (both Linguistics and Craft), and the perception to spot openings. Think of this character as the ultimate manipulator. Their largest goal is to bypass local laws, throw off enemies, and get what the party needs as quickly as they can.<br /> <br /> Some of their techniques include:<br /> <br /> * Passing themselves off as agents of distant nations, or heavenly powers.<br /> <br /> * Starting rumors to throw off the Wyld Hunt and other foes from find the PCs.<br /> <br /> * Forging travel papers, forms of ID, and money depending on location.<br /> <br /> * Creatively securing artifacts, sorcery, and various 'goods'.<br /> <br /> * Mapping out useful agents and cultivating contacts in criminal groups, dissatfied local nobles, and various marginalized peoples.<br /> <br /> * Sabotaging investigations and local governments attempts to derail the PC's plans.<br /> <br /> Consider them Social Sappers. They weaken and break down hostile national and social powers while keeping an eye out for attacks along the same vectors. This mix of sneak thief/social character is quite rewarding to play.<br /> <br /> You'd think a Night would be perfect for this...and they are to a point. But I've seen Twilights and Zeniths pull it off almost better. Just depends on the player's style.<br /> <br /> Yes, Zenith Criminals. It's fun as hell.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:55:35-08:00","id":1661,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 2:52:18 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>The Hilarious Implications of Tiger Training Method -</b><br /> <br /> Ah. Tiger Training Method, so useful, so bizarre, and so exploitable.<br /> <br /> There are other more useful training charms out there that teach other specific skill sets to NPCs, but Tiger Training does 3 things we desperately need.<br /> <br /> 1. It turns mook NPCs into Heroic Mortals.<br /> <br /> 2. It grants them a decent baseline in combat skills.<br /> <br /> 3. It gives them a strong sense of unity and teamwork.<br /> <br /> Ya so? Ya, everything! What they don't tell you is you don't just have to use that charm to turn small villages into trained militia, or your personal followers into a deadly army.<br /> <br /> No, you can use it on ANY social group with the right motivation. That includes your contacts in other areas. So, by quietly using the charm in your down time you can create:<br /> <br /> Kung Fu Cooks, Battle Librarians, and Martial Midwives!<br /> <br /> Own a nice villa. Imagine the surprise when assassins and criminals discover your maid can drop kick them into the other room.<br /> <br /> Think of Tiger Training as Kung Fu Hustle the charm. You can take a impoverished hovel in a city and turn it's residents into some of the most dangerous people around. Transform a brothel into assassins and go all Sin City on the local realm rulers. Pop out trained insurrections in a matter of weeks!<br /> <br /> That's hilariously useful.<br /> <br /> Better yet, set up a dojo as a cover id for your Solar and crank out hyper skilled students that go out and bring in more clients. Given time you can crank out instant armies and battle groups.<br /> <br /> Smooth things over with some social charms and everyone will think it's the way things have always been, and should be!<br /> <br /> Ah Tiger Training. How I love you.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Crumplepunch</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 3:23:49 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> 1. It turns mook NPCs into Heroic Mortals.<br /> <br /> <br /> Does it? I thought it just increased their traits. I was under the impression that you needed to use You Can Be More to make a mortal heroic.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 3:29:21 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Solars Needs No Tools - The MacGyver Way</b><br /> <br /> Craftsmen Needs No Tools, useful, helpful in a tight situations, synergies with solar Excellencies allowing them to churn out useful items with little or no notice. It's application is obvious, right?<br /> <br /> Oh no. Using it just to craft weapons and wagons is just the beginning!<br /> <br /> Read and learn!<br /> <br /> <i>Explorer Needs No Tools -</i><br /> <br /> Craft Earth your way up a mountain or cliff, carving out perfect hand and foot holds AS YOU GO! Make sure to transform a vine and some sticks into perfect guide lines and pitons.<br /> <br /> Craft Water to boil and cook just about anything organic into a delicious stew in seconds. Gator, Claw Strider, Leopard, rocks, tree trunks, whatever. It will taste wonderful and be healthy too.<br /> <br /> Craft Water to eat dangerous plants and spit out poisons into vials.<br /> <br /> Craft Wood to crank out rafts, instant tents, log cabins on the go. Leave safe camps in the worst terrain.<br /> <br /> Craft Earth/Air? to make igloos in minutes.<br /> <br /> Did you know with Craft Earth, Crafting Charms, and maybe a Athletics Charm or 2 you can dig out a underground mine or cave system almost as fast as a Mercury Ant? Neat eh? Secret Base HO!<br /> <br /> Clean and excavate a ruin with your hands safer than with a full crew!<br /> <br /> <i>Socialite Needs No Tools -</i><br /> <br /> Spin sand into perfect glass wear...in your hands. In minutes!<br /> <br /> Repair and clean clothing with a wave of your hand. You ARE the ultimate seamstress.<br /> <br /> Why yes, my ball gown was made from the curtains, and yes it is QUITE stunning!<br /> <br /> No other chef in town can match your home made food.<br /> <br /> Easy Ice Sculptures anyone?<br /> <br /> With the right mix of performance charms your dinner display will be the talk of the town for years to come.<br /> <br /> Instant Costumes!<br /> <br /> Shave, style and dye your hair, brush your teeth. All on the go with your bare hands!<br /> <br /> Snap your fingers and dust leaps off of things!<br /> <br /> Even a shanty town with scrap metal and wood will look like the upper class district of town in a week or less if you are around.<br /> <br /> Peel a Perfect Apple as a stunt, with your fingers!<br /> <br /> Spit and rub out stains on clothing in seconds.<br /> <br /> Grab some incense and make yourself smell like you were bathing in oils for hours.<br /> <br /> One Glass of Water + One bunch of Grapes = Impressive Party Trick.<br /> <br /> Don't have in invitation, mix in some Linguistics charms and YES YOU DO! (Just carry some ink and paper in your pouch.)<br /> <br /> Leave official stamps with your thumb!<br /> <br /> <i>Criminal Needs No Tools -</i><br /> <br /> Lock Disassemble!<br /> <br /> Eyeball Forgeries of rare items with cheap raw materials to crank out great forgeries.<br /> <br /> Cut class with your fingers.<br /> <br /> Saw through walls with the back of your hand.<br /> <br /> Table cloth + random junk = Instant traps.<br /> <br /> Again, perfect costumes!<br /> <br /> The list goes on and on.<br /> <br /> You can think of creative ways to reapply this charm!</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:56:10-08:00","id":1662,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 3:33:37 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Crumplepunch wrote:</b><br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> 1. It turns mook NPCs into Heroic Mortals.<br /> <br /> <br /> Does it? I thought it just increased their traits. I was under the impression that you needed to use You Can Be More to make a mortal heroic.<br /> <br /> <br /> Ah, you are right.<br /> <br /> I forgot they changed that in 2E. I keep forgetting that. Still, Tiger Warriors are NOT mooks and I doubt they would be considered &quot;inconsequential foes&quot; in 3E. So no instant kills.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 3:45:51 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">I hope you all are finding these useful. I'll keep going, just curious about some feedback.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 4:12:02 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <br /> Craft Water to boil and cook just about anything organic into a delicious stew in seconds. Gator, Claw Strider, Leopard, rocks, tree trunks, whatever. It will taste wonderful and be healthy too.<br /> <br /> <br /> I'll just leave <a href=\"http://threepanelsoul.com/2007/05/04/on-alchemy-and-pastries/\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> here.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 4:23:02 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>DeadManSleeping wrote:</b><br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <br /> Craft Water to boil and cook just about anything organic into a delicious stew in seconds. Gator, Claw Strider, Leopard, rocks, tree trunks, whatever. It will taste wonderful and be healthy too.<br /> <br /> <br /> I'll just leave <a href=\"http://threepanelsoul.com/2007/05/04/on-alchemy-and-pastries/\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> here.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 4:31:43 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Have You Considered Living On a Boat?</b><br /> <br /> Frankly I'm surprised that all 1st Age Mansion were not actually artifact boats that Solars were the 'captain' of. No honestly, look at the benefits:<br /> <br /> 1. Charms that allow you to ignore penalties.<br /> 2. Charms that allow you invoke penalties on foes that come on board.<br /> 3. Charms that reinforce your boat against attack.<br /> 4. Charms that improve loyalty of your 'crew' which can include house servants.<br /> 5. Charms that allow you to pilot it around by yourself or a minimum of people.<br /> 6. You can move it as needed.<br /> 7. It doesn't have to be a 'water' boat. You can have a Land Ship/house and still get the benefits.<br /> <br /> So seriously, mobile, magically enforced and defended head quarters.<br /> <br /> You can't tell me your Twilight isn't drooling at the chance to craft a Artifact Boat or Airship of some kind?</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 08:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:56:50-08:00","id":1663,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Gaius</b> - Thursday, June 13, 2013 7:38:37 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">I have neither feedback nor time to formulate any, but:<br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <b>Solars Needs No Tools - The MacGyver Way</b><br /> <i>Gaius thanked this useful post.</i><br /> <br /> Yeah, &quot;nice&quot; is the least I can say of all of them, but that one was very, very fun. ^__^ CNNT suddenly seems like something I'd be interested in touching, were I to ever play a 2e Solar. Doubtful I'd ever play a 2e anything, but hey. May many applications carry on into 3E in some form!</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Drecain</b> - Saturday, June 15, 2013 8:14:35 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <b>Have You Considered Living On a Boat?</b><br /> <br /> Frankly I'm surprised that all 1st Age Mansion were not actually artifact boats that Solars were the 'captain' of. No honestly, look at the benefits:<br /> <br /> 1. Charms that allow you to ignore penalties.<br /> 2. Charms that allow you invoke penalties on foes that come on board.<br /> 3. Charms that reinforce your boat against attack.<br /> 4. Charms that improve loyalty of your 'crew' which can include house servants.<br /> 5. Charms that allow you to pilot it around by yourself or a minimum of people.<br /> 6. You can move it as needed.<br /> 7. It doesn't have to be a 'water' boat. You can have a Land Ship/house and still get the benefits.<br /> <br /> So seriously, mobile, magically enforced and defended head quarters.<br /> <br /> You can't tell me your Twilight isn't drooling at the chance to craft a Artifact Boat or Airship of some kind?<br /> <br /> <br /> I see one problem with this (despite of the general wonderful munchkinness of it, +1 for that ^^) and that's demesnes. IIRC manses cap demesnes and and due to that can't be mobile..<br /> <br /> Edit. You weren't necessarily talking about manses, were you?</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Imrix</b> - Saturday, June 15, 2013 9:37:48 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Drecain wrote:</b><br /> I see one problem with this (despite of the general wonderful munchkinness of it, +1 for that ^^) and that's demesnes. IIRC manses cap demesnes and and due to that can't be mobile..<br /> <br /> Edit. You weren't necessarily talking about manses, were you?<br /> Manses can totally be mobile.<br /> <br /> Heck, some Manses can <i>fly</i>.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: leo1925</b> - Saturday, June 15, 2013 10:11:56 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> 1. No support structures. Lack of allies outside of what you buy in chargen.<br /> <br /> <br /> Well there is the cult of the illuminated that i (really) hope will be a bigger role and have more into it than it did in 2E.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:58:03-08:00","id":1664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Drecain</b> - Monday, June 17, 2013 4:14:07 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Imrix wrote:</b><br /> <b>Drecain wrote:</b><br /> I see one problem with this (despite of the general wonderful munchkinness of it, +1 for that ^^) and that's demesnes. IIRC manses cap demesnes and and due to that can't be mobile..<br /> <br /> Edit. You weren't necessarily talking about manses, were you?<br /> Manses can totally be mobile.<br /> <br /> Heck, some Manses can <i>fly</i>.<br /> <br /> <br /> Really? I get a flying manse along the lines of mount meta that uses the demesne to fly, but if you moved too far from the demesne it's capping wouldn't that render it powerless and make it crash. I mean there are a bunch of rules surrounding what happens to manses with essence buildup when the geomancy changes. Moving around sounds like something that changes geomancy, right? I dunno.. If I was STing, I wouldn't allow anything that is effectively both manse and first age airship...</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 17, 2013 4:17:13 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Drecain wrote:</b><br /> Edit. You weren't necessarily talking about manses, were you?<br /> <br /> <br /> Nope. Mobile base/home only.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Drecain</b> - Monday, June 17, 2013 4:38:02 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Would you agree that a manse-airship is off the table then? Or is it awesome enough to be exalted? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, June 17, 2013 4:47:07 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Drecain wrote:</b><br /> Would you agree that a manse-airship is off the table then? Or is it awesome enough to be exalted? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Manses draw on power from location, so I'd be kosher with a flying Manse, an airship might be a bit much in my games. Now a flying manse where you park your airship between missions....<br /> <br /> But that's me. To each ST their own canon.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:58:35-08:00","id":1665,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Monday, June 17, 2013 4:49:35 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Drecain wrote:</b><br /> Would you agree that a manse-airship is off the table then? Or is it awesome enough to be exalted? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Airship castles are already a thing in Exalted, and floating Manses are already a thing in Exalted, but making a Manse entirely untethered to a location kind of misses the point.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Inugami</b> - Monday, June 17, 2013 8:20:05 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> You can think of creative ways to reapply this charm!<br /> <br /> So much so that I believe it's TOO open-ended and not restricted enough.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 2:17:38 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">]<b>Inugami wrote:</b><br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> You can think of creative ways to reapply this charm!<br /> <br /> So much so that I believe it's TOO open-ended and not restricted enough.<br /> <br /> <br /> Naw. It does one thing very well. Remove the need for tools. Any tools. You still have to have the skill to apply it. It's one of those few charms that interfaces with a wide range of skills and it's application changes over time. It's about perfect in my book.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Inugami</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 2:37:52 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <b>Inugami wrote:</b><br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> You can think of creative ways to reapply this charm!<br /> <br /> So much so that I believe it's TOO open-ended and not restricted enough.<br /> <br /> <br /> Naw. It does one thing very well. Remove the need for tools. Any tools.<br /> <br /> Fire is a tool.<br /> <br /> Solars start fires with a touch?<br /> <br /> More importantly, they can achieve the temperatures necessary to smelt whatever kind of material they're working with?</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 5:10:12 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Inugami wrote:</b><br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> <b>Inugami wrote:</b><br /> <b>Mostlyjoe wrote:</b><br /> You can think of creative ways to reapply this charm!<br /> <br /> So much so that I believe it's TOO open-ended and not restricted enough.<br /> <br /> <br /> Naw. It does one thing very well. Remove the need for tools. Any tools.<br /> <br /> Fire is a tool.<br /> <br /> Solars start fires with a touch?<br /> <br /> More importantly, they can achieve the temperatures necessary to smelt whatever kind of material they're working with?<br /> <br /> <br /> They don't produce fire to smelt steel. In the hands of the Solar Craftsman, the steel becomes tempered without increasing in heat. It is not smelted. It is PERFECTED, made into the right hardness and malleability because the Solar's hands, infused with magic, can guide it as well as the Solar's memories of the forge allow. His knowledge of what to do with tools allows his hands to act in their stead, but not necessarily produce the exact same other effects as them (e.g. heat) except to the extent that such effects are in fact part of the product (e.g. a hot steak).</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T04:59:15-08:00","id":1666,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Nonsensicles</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 5:32:35 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">But you totally could start a fire with a touch if what you were trying to build was a fire. Or your could create a forge capable of producing immense heat with your bare hands. You'd still need fuel for all that heat, though, if you intended the forge to actually be used. Same with the fire, really.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 6:10:45 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">I'm not sure a fire is necessarily a valid objective for a Craft roll (I think it's Survival). Craftsman Needs No Tools may let you take a beehive and string and smash them together to create a candle, but I'm pretty sure that lighting fires with a touch is not the intent of a Charm. I suppose if you were stunting building a seductive living room with a bearskin rug and fireplace, then sure, the fire can be lit without additional effort when you're done, but for the most part, a Solar's hands should not bear fire from his intimacy with crafting. It'd make sense for a Fire Aspect, though (Forge Hands of Hesiesh! Or something).</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Nonsensicles</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 7:12:00 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>DeadManSleeping wrote:</b><br /> I'm not sure a fire is necessarily a valid objective for a Craft roll (I think it's Survival).<br /> <br /> For a simple campfire? Sure. You could roll Survival to cobble that together. Or you could roll Craft (Wood) to build a professional-grade outdoor cooking fire with stone windbreaks.<br /> <br /> Want a ceremonial bonfire? A funeral pyre? A burning effigy? That stuff's all Craft, and can all double as a campfire if it suits you. But it's not complete until you light it, and flint and steel are most certainly tools.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 7:45:05 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Well, for one, a pyre, a fire stack, and an effigy are all completed items before they are put alight.<br /> <br /> For two, it simply doesn't seem appropriate to me that a Solar Exalt goes spewing fire from his hands willy-nilly from a Craft Charm. Like I said, under certain circumstances, a stunt might allow a Solar crafting something specific to easily gloss over lighting the fire, but they shouldn't be spewing fire from their hands just by holding a stick and &quot;crafting&quot; a torch,</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Nonsensicles</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 8:13:32 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">He's not spewing fire willy-nilly. He's making fire happen as the finishing touch of a crafting project, without the need for the tools ordinarily used to spark the fire. Maybe he snaps his fingers, which is sort of like how striking a flint works, and a spark jumps from them into the precise best spot to ignite a good, strong flame. Maybe he blows on it, enflaming non-existent embers to ignite the kindling; embers are a tool too, in that context. Maybe he claps his hands together and it bursts into flame, because that's what the player thinks is cool. Who am I to judge?<br /> <br /> And yeah, you can argue that those things can be considered complete before they're lit, but it's just as valid to say they're only complete when they are. It all depends on whether the craftsman's goal is to build the thing, or to build the fire.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T05:00:03-08:00","id":1667,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Korusef</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 10:04:39 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Nonsensicles wrote:</b><br /> <br /> And yeah, you can argue that those things can be considered complete before they're lit, but it's just as valid to say they're only complete when they are. It all depends on whether the craftsman's goal is to build the thing, or to build the fire.<br /> <br /> <br /> You can build flint &amp; steel (or whatever tools) you need to start the fire .</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Nonsensicles</b> - Tuesday, June 18, 2013 10:29:17 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Korusef wrote:</b><br /> <b>Nonsensicles wrote:</b><br /> <br /> And yeah, you can argue that those things can be considered complete before they're lit, but it's just as valid to say they're only complete when they are. It all depends on whether the craftsman's goal is to build the thing, or to build the fire.<br /> <br /> <br /> You can build flint &amp; steel (or whatever tools) you need to start the fire .<br /> <br /> Maybe. Assuming you have the right Craft for it (Earth for shaping the flint, Fire for the steel) and the physical resources. I don't see what the point is in mentioning that, though. Anything that can be crafted can be crafted. That doesn't mean that Craftsman Needs No Tools can't be used to replace some specific kind of tool.<br /> <br /> Hell, you could ignite a fire with the charm even for Craft (Water). There's nothing in the charm that says you can't use tools if you want, so if you feel like using an actual flame for your cooking, there's no reason you shouldn't be able to ignite it with CNNT. Sure, the flame is a tool, so you can just magically make the effects of it take place, but so is whatever you would normally use to start it. <i>This isn't even all that exploitable.</i> It's just a tiny possible convenience to have a flame you can use for other things when you're done, or more likely a simple bit of dramatic flair because it seems cooler to do it that way.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: DeadManSleeping</b> - Wednesday, June 19, 2013 12:41:27 AM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">&quot;Not that exploitable&quot; isn't an argument to use against people who've had to face this sort of thing while GMing. Trust me, one day it's lighting a campfire, next session it's a house, and pretty soon, you're trying to keep calm as you reject an increasingly loud and troublesome player's assertions that he can use any stick as an unlimited-fuel flamepiece because physics worked that way for him BEFORE so it makes NO SENSE that they can't work that way for him NOW and YOU'RE JUST BEING STUPID and then your whole session is down the toilet and your game isn't going to survive the next few even if you get rid of the player immediately.<br /> <br /> It turns out that we can't have nice things.<br /> <br /> Yes, you run into this problem with a lot of Charms, even if you DO stick to more strict interpretations of the text, but the less rope you give out, the less of it there is to tie a noose for your neck.<br /> <br /> Of course you still run into the &quot;My LAST GM let us do this&quot; player, but that's an entirely different story.<br /> <br /> Do I advocate being an antagonistic GM who never lets the players do anything that the rules don't explicitly allow? No. But ruling that an interpretation is a necessarily logical extension when it opens up a wide range of possibilities that are probably outside the intent of the mechanic is a bad road to go down. It's a mistake I've made. It's a mistake I've coaxed GMs into (man, I haven't been a non-GM in a while, now that I think on it). It's dangerous.<br /> <br /> It's not like any Twilight worth anything won't find a way to set everything on fire whenever he wants anyway.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Thursday, June 20, 2013 2:39:10 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>DeadManSleeping wrote:</b><br /> &quot;Not that exploitable&quot; isn't an argument to use against people who've had to face this sort of thing while GMing.<br /> <br /> <br /> I follow the rules of Yes, but. I want to provide creative applications of charms that folks may not have thought of. CNNT is flexible, but it's not unbalancing. You still have to have raw materials. It does not do away with that requirement. But you can stunt a lot of what you need. Then again, you could STUNT what CNNT does anyway. CNNT just removes the need to stunt and opens up weirder applications if you think about it. And the 'fire' or heat a user makes is towards the crafted item, it can not normally be used as an offence. It sure as hell doesn't work in combat terms. Like say a Fire Aspect's anima flux if that's what you are thinking.<br /> <br /> You do it your way, but that's not the point of this thread. If you don't want to allow some of the wider applications of the charm. Fine, it's your game.<br /> <br /> I'll post more ideas down the line, been sick all week.<br /> <br /> Nonsensicles is right. The 'fire' is a stunt element in narrative form btw. So manual THANK.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T05:00:41-08:00","id":1668,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, July 01, 2013 3:51:38 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>It's all about building a family</b><br /> <br /> Or more correctly a Dynasty. One trick my PC's have used in prior games that pays off down the line with loyalty and growing your personal army of contacts, allies, and followers is the idea of the extended family. You know the concept of 'adopting the kobold right?' Same application but with some interesting differences thanks to how Solar's power work.<br /> <br /> First off, ever thought of using a local DB as your party's Face? Solars who befriend a non-Dynast or friendly Dynast (hey, it happens) can use the DB as their forward speaker. Especially if the DB is treated right, listened too, and allowed to have sway on party activity. Remember, Dragon Blooded tend to have just as much raw skill as a Solar just not the crazy high power level to apply. If you have a DB with massive social skills, let him or her lead the conversation with Solar's acting as passive support. In many ways charm use might get ignored with the DB acting as the distraction from the rest of the party. And it's impressive as hell when a previously minor local Dragon Blood shows up with Sorcery, social, and technical powers at his or her command.<br /> <br /> Feeding your party Lunar shapes has benefits. Sure, they have to run the sacred hunt, but acting as a spotter, and assisting in the hunt isn't a bad thing. Or better, looking for and creating ways for the Lunar's 'new' face to enter a social situation allows for crazy good deep cover. Solars are the ultimate social monkeywrenches, and a lot of their charms if used to distract and sooth social questions and fears can take a Lunar ally and embed them in just about any location with little to no questions. One of the great synergies of Lunar to Solar is Lunars sneak why Solars distract. Use this to your advantage.<br /> <br /> Etc, etc. The idea is to look at other Exalts and morals as additions to your character. Remember, contacts and allies are the great balances for you own power or skill holes. You don't have to be good at everything, even though some Solar players think this is the case.<br /> <br /> In the idea of adopting others into your family, it can be literally. While the Solars of old use to be the kings and queens of creation nothing says you have to play it by that book. If anything you can change the story up and play a more background role to a larger mix of allies. Sure they would be expecting you to be the big walking target, but you don't have to be. You can have a crazy group of consorts, heirs, aunts, and uncles, all part of your massive functional clan.<br /> <br /> In fact it might be wonderful game idea. A small Solar circle, finding lone Exalts with no allies or friends and bringing them into the extended social order you yourself are building. Placing them with responsibility, and trust from your core group and then spending the next 10 play sessions dealing with the weird mix of attitudes your new cousins and friends present. If what I'm saying sounds like a non-aggressive touchy feeling game, then you got it in one.<br /> <br /> No reason why you have to win over creation with the Daiklave. Win it over with trust. It's a fun way to play the game too.<br /> <br /> <b>tl:dr: Don't be the king. Be the papa/mama for your family of allies and let them shine while you support their goals. </b><b>Just pick your family carefully.</b></td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, July 01, 2013 4:11:25 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Who is John &quot;Solar&quot;? (NPC idea)</b><br /> <br /> Not a call for Solar Objectivism, but rather the idea of chasing an NPC Solar who keeps crossing the PC's path. This 'bait' NPC does things Similar to the PC's actions. Maybe not directly influencing their NPC cats pays in the Guild, but maybe mounting a Wagon Rider's strike against bad wages. Or A local leader is willing to deal with the PCs because the prior Solar he dealt with gave him such a great deal. Etc.<br /> <br /> The point of this Galtish Solar is to guide the PC's attention in a more subtle method than the &quot;Fixer&quot; from prior examples. Especially if the group is ignoring a critical plot point in the game the ST would like to draw to their attention. Because nothing draws PC's attention in like another of their rare kind.<br /> <br /> The John Solar doesn't have to be a foe, but he needs to be hard to reach. The group needs to invest time and effort to track the NPC down. Along the way stumbling over his/her efforts and plans. Offer the PC's a chance to meddle themselves, to pick up plot points, and get hooked into sub plots you have written up.<br /> <br /> Eventually they find John Solar and he can be the big bad for the game, a patsy for a more powerful foe, or even a potential NPC ally that shows up randomly in the plot.<br /> <br /> Your choice. Either way, John Solar needs to be like Carmen Santiago and always on the move. Make em explore and work for those meetings.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, July 01, 2013 4:25:11 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Social Armor? Or Choose Your Battlefield!</b><br /> <br /> Tired of being ambushed? Want to disrupt a foe in a creative and interesting ways? How about setting them up, stacking the dice, inviting them to a PARTY!<br /> <br /> The first rule of social fight club is Mastery of Small Manners and Social Imposition forces your foes to follow codes of conduct and behavior when facing you in social situations that you set up. So find a favorite nephew of your Guild ally who's having a birthday party and invite you FOE! Worse yet, empower your invitation with Linguistic charms and Social charms.<br /> <br /> Hated foe shows up, congratulates the child, gives gifts, and has to sit there with a smile on the whole party while you have your terse social meeting with them.<br /> <br /> Even better? Invite them to a college (school) event and 'invite' them to play Truth or Dare. Gambling, placing bets? Why those follow social rules and conventions too! Travel around, learn the weird little bylaws of the land and use them to set up scenes where foes and friends alike are bolstered or hindered to your choosing while you have friendly, or not so friendly chats with them.<br /> <br /> Man, nothing like winning a 'fight' with out lifting a hand eh?</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-03T05:01:17-08:00","id":1669,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, July 01, 2013 5:12:09 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Okay. Enough being nice:<br /> <br /> <b>CRISIS ENTRY</b> (Note this works in any game, but Solars do it with <i>style</i>.) (Sometimes also called &quot;Dynamic Entry&quot;)<br /> <br /> Abyssal foe is hold up in his Manse HQ. He's got armies of skeletons, traps and secure bolt holds leading in and out of his fortress and expects you to walk that mile of hell just to have an audience...<br /> <br /> ...screw that noise!<br /> <br /> Cloud Skiff your way in from above it, dropping your 2 Solars after you've Radiant Raptored a weak patrol to wipe the floor with everyone. With Blood Apes on each side of you as you slowly walk into the dust and blood while doing presence attacks on everyone in sight to make them BOW.<br /> <br /> Let me put it to you this way. If you are not entering a scene or battlefield like Darth Vader enters Rebel Starships, you are doing it WRONG. (Typically, Night Castes are funny that way.)<br /> <br /> Think of it this way. If you ever can get the drop on someone, or you are plotting an attack on their location, or you just want people to understand that you are the most dangerous player in town (even if you are not) you have to have a bit of showiness. Even if Anima banners are not used.<br /> <br /> Bandit camp? Burst in with explosions and Performance Charms and then use Rout Stemming Gesture to stop them from running away! No seriously. Make them stop and LOOK at you. Smile. Stunt that tooth gleem. See a location? Don't ever go in through the front door, avoid the back door if you can do so, Find a wall and TAKE IT DOWN, or if you have to...open up the ceiling. One of my players earned the title &quot;DOOR BANE&quot; because damn it, he hated using doors. (Not really, but it's the rumor that came out of a misunderstanding in game.)<br /> <br /> Invest in charms that allow you to walk up walls, fly in using sorcery, knock down gates with your fingers, special effects!<br /> <br /> <i>Blind people, cause chaos, specialize in it! One part commando operation, one part fashion show. That's what being a Solar is about!</i><br /> <br /> And that brings me to the Solar type that is all this:<br /> <br /> <b>The Showman Solar</b><br /> <br /> Some Solars are impressive just by what they do. Some Solars take looking impressive to the level beyond that. That's you...the expert in looking cool. Part Con Man, part entertainer, you gimmick is first, second, and last impressions. You always look good, you always have people on hand to help with costumes and effects, you invest in showy magic, you don't try to hide, you are a walking Exalted target. Why? Because if they are paying attention to you, they are NOT paying attention to the rest of your party as they muck things up.<br /> <br /> Just fold in some suitability charms as in dodge, parry, etc. Just in case. And then mince you way across the battlefield to get the generals drooling. Only you can make the blood stained armor look this good.</td> </tr> </table></div> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\"><b>Posted by: Mostlyjoe</b> - Monday, July 01, 2013 5:31:22 PM</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Here's a shorty but cutie.<br /> <br /> <b>Weaponize your Past Lives!</b><br /> <br /> Did you know Solars fought against all sorts of horrors and monsters in the eons they use to rule Creation, or worse things before they took over?<br /> <br /> Did you know that one of the 'easiest' ways to nab some bonus dice in a combat situation is the flashback to a previous fight that was much like this one.<br /> <br /> Or if the foe you are fighting fought your past self, have a Anima 'trick' of a ghostly image of your past self show up to frighten your foe in the middle of the fight.<br /> <br /> The key here is to remember, literally EVERYTHING about your character's back story can be used in some fashion in stunting, or social to physical combat tricks. Think about what you have going on in you plot and figure out ways to weaponize it.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","user":"Drecain","date":". 11-03-2013, 09:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/327-drecain","name":"Drecain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"216"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}